        "content": "# SkillForge Project Information\n\n**Creation Date:** August 4, 2026\n\n**Description:** SkillForge is a \"local-first skill runtime for AI Coding Agents\" that defines skills as \"verifiable, composable, MCP-exposable independent units.\" It enables agents to reuse capabilities like software packages while constraining read/write access through whitelist sandboxes.\n\n**What It Does:** SkillForge provides an infrastructure system for managing agent skills with structured manifests (skill.yml), execution boundaries (allow.read_paths, allow.write_paths, allow.net_hosts), and reusable entry points. It includes a Rust CLI with run/serve/validate commands, strict skill.yml validation, whitelist-based local file I/O sandboxing, macOS Seatbelt platform sandbox integration, a Plan-Act-Observe-Reflect execution flow, skill evaluation with CI gates, token budget management, and MCP protocol exposure. The system also features a TypeScript Registry CLI supporting local search/publish/install and GitHub Release distribution.\n\n**Release Status:** The repository is in \"Phase 1 MVP stage\" with core capabilities fully implemented, including the Rust CLI, registry components (CLI, HTTP service MVP, and Next.js web UI), and example skills (fix-ci, grep-ts, doc-gen). The project is publicly available under the Apache License 2.0 and has 106 GitHub stars.\n\n**Launch Information:** SkillForge is currently in active development as a Phase 1 MVP. The project documentation states it's \"ready to run\" with complete local and remote MVP pathways, though it notes additional features are needed for \"production-grade platform for organizations of any scale.\" The roadmap includes planned enhancements such as S3 storage backend, real database migration from SQLite to Postgres, and expanded web UI capabilities."
